1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,900
Dad, what's Nuri doing right now?

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:05,200
Taking pictures in space! Throwing up a peace sign, too!

3
00:00:05,367 --> 00:00:09,133
Sir, Nuri isn't a camera. It's a delivery truck.

4
00:00:09,133 --> 00:00:10,300
…Huh?

5
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:15,067
Wrong answer number one. I'm Modapman.

6
00:00:16,633 --> 00:00:24,867
Case summary. The weekend after the Nuri launch news. Location: a space science museum.

7
00:00:24,867 --> 00:00:28,033
Satellites just float up there, right? Zero gravity.

8
00:00:28,033 --> 00:00:30,733
They don't float. They're falling. Right now.

9
00:00:30,733 --> 00:00:35,033
Wha? Wha? Wha? Wha… AAAH!

10
00:00:35,067 --> 00:00:40,900
Wrong answer, two. Mom married into the Mo family after all.

11
00:00:40,933 --> 00:00:43,667
Then what about spaceships? How are they different from satellites?

12
00:00:43,667 --> 00:00:48,400
A spaceship's just a big satellite! Next vacation, we ride Nuri to space!

13
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:50,533
Nuri doesn't have seats for people.

14
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:57,233
Wrong answer, three. The Mo family's space program: cancelled.

15
00:00:57,267 --> 00:00:59,067
Okay. Just three rounds.

16
00:00:59,067 --> 00:01:00,800
The museum has rounds too…?

17
00:01:00,833 --> 00:01:05,167
Round one. Nuri is a rocket. A launch vehicle, to be exact.

18
00:01:05,167 --> 00:01:08,000
A delivery truck that carries satellites up to space.

19
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,233
Each section drops off when its fuel runs out. Stages one and two fall into the sea.

20
00:01:12,233 --> 00:01:13,967
The truck falls off?!

21
00:01:13,967 --> 00:01:15,733
Then who's taking the pictures?

22
00:01:15,733 --> 00:01:19,700
The satellites it carried. This time, five little ones.

23
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:21,900
They sent back their first pictures in less than a day.

24
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:25,167
Nuri is the truck. The satellite's the photographer. Noted.

25
00:01:25,167 --> 00:01:28,233
Noted correctly. For the first time.

26
00:01:28,267 --> 00:01:31,033
Round two. Satellites are actually falling. All the time.

27
00:01:31,033 --> 00:01:34,867
But they're zooming sideways. Over seven kilometers a second.

28
00:01:34,867 --> 00:01:39,000
As they fall, the round Earth curves away beneath them, so they never hit the ground. They just keep circling.

29
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,700
Not floating… falling. Nonstop.

30
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:44,800
So it falls super fast and keeps missing!

31
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:48,300
Exactly. That's why one lap around Earth takes about an hour and a half.

32
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:49,933
One lap before school's even out!

33
00:01:49,967 --> 00:01:54,233
Round three. Anything that circles a planet is a satellite.

34
00:01:54,233 --> 00:01:55,233
Then the Moon too?

35
00:01:55,233 --> 00:01:58,600
The Moon's a satellite too. A natural one nobody built.

36
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:00,333
The Moon was a satellite this whole time?!

37
00:02:00,433 --> 00:02:04,433
Build one and launch it, it's an artificial satellite. People ride it, it's a spaceship.

38
00:02:04,433 --> 00:02:07,467
Nuri only carries cargo, so there are no seats.

39
00:02:07,467 --> 00:02:08,633
Not even a window seat…?

40
00:02:08,633 --> 00:02:09,767
There aren't even windows.

41
00:02:09,867 --> 00:02:18,000
In one line: rockets deliver, satellites circle and work, spaceships carry people.

42
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,367
Nuri isn't a satellite. It's the rocket that lifted the satellites up.

43
00:02:23,533 --> 00:02:28,000
Nuri carried the satellites on its back and ran all the way to the edge of the sky.

44
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,400
Then it let go, one piece at a time, and went back down to the sea.

45
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:34,000
It doesn't get to go all the way?

46
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,267
Nope. But the satellites are still circling, never resting, taking pictures of our land.

47
00:02:39,267 --> 00:02:42,433
So Nuri is the one who drops them off.

48
00:02:42,433 --> 00:02:45,500
Yep. And that's the hardest job of all.

49
00:02:45,533 --> 00:02:50,733
My kid gave the sky a little wave.

50
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:54,200
One lap's an hour and a half, right? Then it'll pass right over us soon!

51
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:57,033
The Earth turns in the meantime, so it'll pass somewhere else.

52
00:02:57,033 --> 00:02:59,800
…I'll wait with a peace sign anyway.

53
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,267
Dad, the satellites' first pictures were of America!

54
00:03:02,267 --> 00:03:08,300
Dad held a peace sign in the parking lot for an hour and a half.

55
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:12,600
Wrong answer, four. Modapman strikes again.
